Subject: [RFC PATCH] drm/i915/dsi: Allow overriding the LINK_READY timeout
X-Patch-License: MIT

The gen11+ DSI enable sequence waits up to 2500 us for LINK_READY.
On a Raptor Lake-U system, panel bring-up exceeds this wait and produces
visible display corruption. A suspend/resume cycle restores normal output.

Temporary instrumentation observed LINK_READY at 2702.432..2707.946 us
from entry to the register wait in thirty panel-enable cycles. During a
boot with the normal polling backoff, the bit was still clear at
2560.080 us and was first observed set at 4825.256 us. These software
observations motivate a longer wait; they are not electrical timing
measurements or proof of the issue on every DSI implementation.

Add a load-time dsi_link_ready_timeout_us display parameter to permit
an explicit override without rebuilding the driver. Keep the existing
2500 us default and polling mechanism. Clamp the effective value to
2500..100000 us to prevent shorter waits and arbitrarily long timeouts.
The maximum is a proposed policy limit, not a hardware-specification value.

Do not add PCI or DMI matching. The wait completes when LINK_READY is
observed instead of unconditionally sleeping for the selected timeout.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
LOCAL RFC DRAFT. Not submitted. A human author and DCO sign-off must be
supplied after review; no Signed-off-by or Fixes tag has been invented.

Base: torvalds/linux 32c52ec87d6245ee2d12ed5dd509e40062939b50

Testing and scope:
- Timing data and runtime validation use downstream CachyOS, not vanilla
  mainline or drm-tip. The timing instrumentation was removed afterwards.
- Historical downstream parameter-based modules were tested at 10000 us
  for boot, panel-enable cycles and S3. A separate 5000 us trial passed
  one reboot and one S3/deep cycle; the owner confirmed clean output
  before suspend and after resume. Binary hashes were unchanged during
  that trial.
- On 2026-10-21, the packaged CachyOS 7.2.11-1-cachyos i915 driver, without
  the custom timeout parameter, passed one power-off/button-start test and
  one supervised S3/deep suspend/resume cycle with an early 60 Hz VBT
  override. The owner confirmed clean output and correct orientation;
  the captured kernel journals contain no DSI link-ready failure.
- Those stock-driver results apply to one U300 and panel. They do not
  validate this RFC, establish repeated cold-start reliability, or prove
  that changing refresh rate fixes every instance of the timeout issue.
- On matching hardware, try the locally tested experimental 60 Hz mode
  with stock i915 first. This unbuilt RFC remains a developer investigation
  if that mode is unsuitable or startup corruption persists. No cold-start test
  of the parameter-based patched module was performed.
- This exact upstream-base patch passes apply/whitespace/checkpatch checks.
  It has not been compiled or boot-tested; i915/xe build coverage is pending.
- The available working-boot log uses normal verbosity. A fresh failing
  upstream baseline with full DRM debug logging is still missing.

Design questions for the reviewers:
- Is a permanent opt-in module parameter appropriate, or is there a
  correct automatic timeout/enable-sequence fix for this condition?
- What requirement determines the current 2500 us bound?
- Is 100000 us a suitable maximum? Should invalid values be rejected
  rather than clamped?

 drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/icl_dsi.c              | 4 +++-
 drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.c | 3 +++
 drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.h | 1 +
 3 files changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/icl_dsi.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/icl_dsi.c
index ea0cdb7822f39f4d46e13f63858ab78055aed4f4..87fc3ae6d552dc793ab032074808369778128d67 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/icl_dsi.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/icl_dsi.c
@@ -858,13 +858,15 @@ gen11_dsi_configure_transcoder(struct intel_encoder *encoder,
 
 	/* wait for link ready */
 	for_each_dsi_port(port, intel_dsi->ports) {
+		unsigned int timeout_us = clamp_val(display->params.dsi_link_ready_timeout_us,
+						    2500, 100000);
 		int ret;
 
 		dsi_trans = dsi_port_to_transcoder(port);
 
 		ret = intel_de_wait_for_set_us(display,
 					       DSI_TRANS_FUNC_CONF(dsi_trans),
-					       LINK_READY, 2500);
+					       LINK_READY, timeout_us);
 		if (ret)
 			drm_err(display->drm, "DSI link not ready\n");
 	}
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.c
index ca0ef466bb1039fb3600d9b2136f639ba9e39a5a..b57f19fd0be3e52585b1b92500eafe484126fd0e 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/intel_display_params.c
@@ -143,6 +143,9 @@ intel_display_param_named_unsafe(enable_dmc_wl, int, 0400,
 	"(-1=use per-chip default, 0=disabled, 1=enabled, 2=match any register, 3=always locked) "
 	"Default: -1");
 
+intel_display_param_named_unsafe(dsi_link_ready_timeout_us, uint, 0400,
+				 "DSI LINK_READY timeout (us; default 2500; clamped 2500..100000)");
+
 __maybe_unused
 static void _param_print_bool(struct drm_printer *p, const char *driver_name,
 			      const char *name, bool val)
